## 1. Overview & Problem Definition

Phase I implements the **Spatial, Compound and Common-Mode Reliability Specialist (`SPATIAL_RELIABILITY_V1`)**, expanding Veyra from single-location hazard specialists to networked spatial failure graph intelligence across 25 canonical Indian meteorological stations.
In accordance with Veyra's non-negotiable architectural principles:
- Spatial propagation represents empirical spatio-temporal covariance of forecast errors, never physical causality.
- Compound hazard failure probabilities are evaluated as explicit joint sets under Fréchet-Hoeffding copula bounds ($P(A \cap B) \le \min(P_A, P_B)$), never as an unexplained average score.
- Rainfall reliability is strictly distinct from hydrological flood probability (`hydrological_flood_claim: false`, `rainfall_reliability_distinct_from_flood: true`).
- Anti-leakage at $t_0$: only issue-time atmospheric state enters inference; station ground truth is sealed behind a 24h verification latency.
- Full Level 1-4 baseline ladder comparison.
- Leave-one-region-out (LORO) hold-out validation across 7 macro-regions.
- Common-mode failure detection with Common-Mode Severity Index (CMSI) and ablation testing.

